# Cursor Rule: Icon Usage with Nuxt Icon

## Purpose
Standardize the way icons are added and used in this project using the Nuxt Icon package.

---

## 1. Icon Library
- **Use only the [Nuxt Icon](https://github.com/nuxt-modules/icon) package** for all icon needs.
- Do not import SVGs or use other icon libraries directly in components.

---

## 2. Installation
If not already installed, add Nuxt Icon to your project:
```bash
pnpm add @nuxt/icon
# or
npm install @nuxt/icon
```
Add the module to your `nuxt.config.ts`:
```ts
export default defineNuxtConfig({
  modules: [
    '@nuxt/icon',
    // ...other modules
  ],
})
```

---

## 3. Usage
- Use the `<NuxtIcon />` component to render icons.
- Reference icons by their name as registered in Nuxt Icon.
- Example usage in a Vue file:
```vue
<template>
  <NuxtIcon name="mdi:home" size="24" />
</template>
```

---

## 4. Naming Conventions
- Use the full icon name as required by Nuxt Icon (e.g., `mdi:home`, `fa:check`).
- For custom icons, register them according to Nuxt Icon documentation and use a consistent prefix (e.g., `custom:my-icon`).

---

## 5. Best Practices
- Prefer semantic icon names that match their purpose (e.g., `mdi:delete` for a delete button).
- Use the `size` prop for sizing instead of custom CSS when possible.
- Avoid inline SVGs or external icon fonts.
- Document any new custom icons in the project README or a dedicated icon reference file.

---

## 6. Example: Button with Icon
```vue
<template>
  <button>
    <NuxtIcon name="mdi:plus" size="20" />
    Add Item
  </button>
</template>
```

---

## 7. Linting & Review
- PRs adding icons should follow this rule.
- Reviewers: Ensure only Nuxt Icon is used for new icons.

---

